You check a research paper against a reproducibility checklist. Use only the text below
(experiment sections, tables and appendix). Every paragraph or table starts with an id in
brackets.

For EACH checklist item below, return one entry:
- "id": the item id exactly as given.
- "status":
  - "present": the paper fully states this for its MAIN experiments and models.
  - "partial": the paper states only some of it. Typical cases: training time or hardware
    for one model or one experiment only; GPU type without the number of devices (or the
    reverse); standard deviations or seeds for some results but not the main ones;
    hyperparameters for the proposed method but not the baselines; some preprocessing
    steps only.
  - "missing": the text below does not state it.
  - "not_applicable": the item cannot apply to this paper (e.g. no baselines are compared).
  A single passing mention is "partial", not "present". When unsure between the two,
  choose "partial".
- "quote": for present/partial, ONE sentence (or one table caption/row) copied
  character-for-character from the text that shows it. Empty for missing/not_applicable.
- "chunk_id": the id in brackets of the paragraph or table the quote comes from, or empty.
- "note": one short sentence explaining the status (what is stated, or what is missing).

Judge only from the text. Do not assume something is stated because it is common practice.
Do not compute anything.

